00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:57.519
And other things including the Q&amp;A document.

00:00:57.519 --> 00:01:00.489
Let's get started with the content.

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:04.080
So we will talk about using it the web BLAST
tabular format.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.540
My email address is there, peter.cooper@nih.gov.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.780
If you have questions about this material,
we will save those for a different email address.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.910
So what we will do today is learn how to download
the hit table from the BLAST results.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:23.270
We will use the Unix / Linux tools to process
tabular output.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:28.180
We are also use EDirect to get additional
information on database matches.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:33.941
And we will combine new information back into
a tabular format so you can basically add

00:01:33.941 --> 00:01:37.060
columns to the tab format to get more information.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:42.160
We will show you at the end, if you had a
lot of sequences in your BLAST output, and

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.831
you want to download them and we will show
you how to do them with the EDirect program

00:01:45.831 --> 00:01:47.510
efetch.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:51.630
So we know that many people are interested
in saving BLAST results.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:02:00.610
In the old days I know that many cases people
would literally print out the pairwise format

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.130
and save it in a notebook.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.690
This is no longer an option.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.860
And it is not a good idea anyway.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:12.740
But it is useful to save this stuff in some
format that you can archive.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.800
There are several options here.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.830
The one we will focus on today is a real nice
option because it is compact.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.700
Is called the hit table format.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.950
It contains abbreviated information.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.910
Of course there are structured outputs.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.070
That is for more advanced audiences.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:35.920
If you want to deal with that and you have
more advanced ways of dealing with it, that's

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:36.920
great.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.060
XML is a good choice and there is a webinar
on the YouTube channel on how to use the EDirect

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.490
program xtract to parse that output.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:50.550
We will talk today about the hit table format
and how to deal with.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:56.630
Basically what you get with the hit table
text, there is as a CSV comma separated format.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:00.500
It's the same stuff without headers.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.959
It does not have your columns labeled.

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:05.430
Many people would probably prefer this one.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.362
The advantage of this format and this is what
a lot of people do with this.

00:03:08.362 --> 00:03:13.569
It is easily opened and manipulated with spreadsheet
software like Excel.

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:18.580
It is a compact file.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:24.080
The problem is it is abbreviated.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.790
It doesn't have sequence titles.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.630
It doesn't have taxonomy information.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:31.660
It doesn't have the links to get match sequences.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:36.080
We will see how to add all that in a few minutes.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.130
The way we are going to do that is we are
going to use simple UNIX command line tools

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:43.440
for working with text files and columns.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.680
There are a whole ton of these.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.030
The ones we will use today are cut and paste.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:50.030
And sort.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.180
They are all the names you can use a command
line.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.560
There is a programming language called awk.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.940
A world unto itself.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.310
The ancestor of languages like Perl.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:05.610
We will use it in a very limited way today.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:12.810
Right now if you are in an advanced scripter
and you know a lot about UNIX, this probably

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:13.810
is not for you.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.080
You know more about this and I do.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.049
This is for people who have modest needs to
deal with this.

00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:21.479
I will show you some simple ways to do it.

00:04:21.479 --> 00:04:28.469
We are also using EDirect, the programs efetch,
epost, elink and xtract.

00:04:28.469 --> 00:04:32.840
You may say yourself what if I don't have
a UNIX / Linux machine?

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.229
That's okay.

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:39.389
If you have a Windows machine, you can use
the application cygwin when which you can

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:41.099
get from web download.

00:04:41.099 --> 00:04:44.650
It is basically a Linux like environment.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:47.139
You can work with all these program.

00:04:47.139 --> 00:04:51.489
On Mac you do have a UNIX Linux system.

00:04:51.489 --> 00:04:54.610
You can get to it from the Terminal application.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:58.259
So I'll just take a moment to mention Edirect.

00:04:58.259 --> 00:05:00.300
A commandline version of the E-utilities.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.060
We've given webinars on this and there is
lots of good information available in the

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.819
documentation on the bookshelf.

00:05:07.819 --> 00:05:10.999
And we are going to use that and it is installed
on our machines here.

00:05:10.999 --> 00:05:17.379
You can easily install on your Windows or
Mac or Linux machine.

00:05:17.379 --> 00:05:22.830
We will learn how to use them briefly.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.559
Basically here are the different programs.

00:05:24.559 --> 00:05:28.490
They all interact with the history server
at NCBI.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.069
You can post some IDs up there.

00:05:30.069 --> 00:05:32.169
They could come from a search.

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:35.319
You retrieve the IDs in whatever format you
want.

00:05:35.319 --> 00:05:38.310
Most commonly that retrieval is in the form
of an XML record.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:43.620
Then you use xtract to parse out things from
the XML.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.759
We will do that today.

00:05:46.759 --> 00:05:50.669
There is a webinar on this that you can see
on our YouTube channel as well.

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:54.289
So typically EDirect process would be something
like this.

00:05:54.289 --> 00:05:57.469
You have IDs.

00:05:57.469 --> 00:06:01.860
You can get them from the program esearch,
we won't talk about esearch today, but say

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:06.669
we have some IDs and we put them in efetch.

00:06:06.669 --> 00:06:11.639
And we pipe that to the other program, xtract.

00:06:11.639 --> 00:06:15.710
And will try to get some information using
this parser xtract.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:22.169
If you have lots of IDs, we might want to
use epost and to put them in the history server,

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:26.229
retrieve them and use xtract to parse them
out.

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:30.759
Likewise, we could get the links from those
say we want to have our protein records linked

00:06:30.759 --> 00:06:37.029
to PubMed we can follow the links and get
the information for the PubMed records using

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:39.369
xtract after we fetch them.

00:06:39.369 --> 00:06:43.499
When you extend this whole process dealing
with BLAST output you are basically going

00:06:43.499 --> 00:06:50.509
to take the tab-delimited output we have and
we are going to do things with it with awk,

00:06:50.509 --> 00:06:51.910
cut, paste, and sort.

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:56.409
We will pass that to EDirect and we will take
the data we get back from edirect and put

00:06:56.409 --> 00:07:02.289
back into the tabular output using the UNIX
command line programs.

00:07:02.289 --> 00:07:09.060
Here is a complicated example that I hope
to get to today where we are basically going

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:12.439
to take her BLAST output.

00:07:12.439 --> 00:07:14.789
Get some information out of it using awk.

00:07:14.789 --> 00:07:18.020
Post those IDs to efetch.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.080
Will pull them down and get some information
with xtract.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:25.870
We will sort that information by a particular
column.

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:32.949
And we will take that file and paste an output
stream into it and get a much larger tabular

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:35.889
delimited file and use cut to get rid of an
extra column.

00:07:35.889 --> 00:07:39.939
That is a workflow you could do that uses
a bunch of different tools.

00:07:39.939 --> 00:07:43.271
There are the EDirect programs here at the
beginning.

00:07:43.271 --> 00:07:47.439
A bunch of postprocessing using UNIX commandline
tools.

00:07:47.439 --> 00:07:48.439
So here's what we will do.

00:07:48.439 --> 00:07:52.789
We are going to use awk to add links from
BLAST matches to protein records at NCBI.

00:07:52.789 --> 00:07:55.699
We will do some stuff with EDirect.

00:07:55.699 --> 00:08:02.240
We will use epost, efetch, and xtract to get
sequence titles, taxonomy IDs.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.879
Will use UNIX sort paste and cut to process
output.

00:08:05.879 --> 00:08:11.629
Will take a brief look at some other applications
so we can get some publications and some taxonomy

00:08:11.629 --> 00:08:13.370
information from her BLAST output.

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:19.429
And will use efetch and sequence formats to
download fasta proteins.

00:08:19.429 --> 00:08:25.909
If time permits I will point out to you the
nucleotide coding region output you can get

