I could use it later on if I wanted to go
into the taxonomy database.

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:27.430
How do I do this for all of my IDs?

00:16:27.430 --> 00:16:29.759
I need to use epost.

00:16:29.759 --> 00:16:36.800
Epost has a real simple sort of syntax.

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:44.439
You can always see the options for any of
the EDirect programs by typing help at the

00:16:44.439 --> 00:16:45.809
end.

00:16:45.809 --> 00:16:54.050
That is all the things I could do with epost
I can take a list of IDs.

00:16:54.050 --> 00:16:56.059
put them in the history then work on them.

00:16:56.059 --> 00:16:59.600
The command line that will do that for you
with all of the IDs.

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:05.990
It's just taking the exact same awk command
that we used before to get those IDs from

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:06.990
the file.

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:15.690
And then I will parse all this information
out using xtract.

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:18.960
This is going to probably wrap.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:23.210
Here they all are.

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:27.240
Now I have all this information.

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:30.250
I could paste it into my file but you might
notice something.

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:37.290
That is that these things are in a different
order than they are in the in the XL file.

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:39.410
What I have to do is sort these by something.

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:42.780
Then I can sort the Excel file in the same
way.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:45.110
And at this back in.

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:51.390
So we are just going to use the UNIX command
to sort.

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:55.700
So the first column there is the accession.version.

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:59.170
I can sort by that.

00:17:59.170 --> 00:18:08.131
So I have just piped the output here.

00:18:08.131 --> 00:18:10.520
Here we are doing the work flow.

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:13.460
We are starting with a UNIX command line tool.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:21.750
We are going into edirect and finishing with
a UNIX command line tool sort.

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:23.140
Now these are sorted by accession.

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:29.500
We do this because what I want to do is to
go back in and sort the other tabular output

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:35.740
by accession so I can get the body of that
table to line up.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:40.430
So I can take the exact same command line
that had before and just add sort.

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:42.510
Now I am going to have to sort by the second
column.

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:49.640
Because that is where the identifier I am
interested in is.

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:51.070
I can save it if I want to.

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:53.690
I will save that for us.

00:18:53.690 --> 00:19:00.070
I will use a redirection output operator sign
there, the greater than, to put that into

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:01.860
a file.

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:08.000
It did not take long because it was local
data that I had.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:09.890
Notice we are just processing the file.

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:12.340
We are not doing anything here.

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:20.960
I can just more that if you want.

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:25.440
Now it is sorted by accession and exactly
the same way that the output was a minute

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:26.440
ago.

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:32.960
Now there is a long command line as the final
version of this where I take these two things

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:36.320
and paste them together to make this output.

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:44.200
You could also simply take this output here,
copy it, well first you want to sort your

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.980
spreadsheet by this column, the accession.

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:53.550
Once you do that, you could paste this into
Excel and get your additional columns.

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:54.960
The first one into Excel.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.710
This is what you're sorted Excel spreadsheet
would look like.

00:19:57.710 --> 00:20:03.240
I can do that computationally or on the computer.

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:10.010
By combining a bunch of different things.

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:13.160
This will get a little ugly because it is
going to be a long table now.

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:16.650
I have added another UNIX command at the end
of this.

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:18.800
That is the program paste.

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:24.560
Paste will take this sorted hit table text
file, and that little dash says standard output.

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:32.470
I will take this stream here just retrieving
that information that we did a few minutes

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:33.470
ago.

00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:39.600
To get the additional information from efetch.

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:43.710
I will pipe this into more.

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:48.030
And now we have, it is kind of funny to watch
because it's wrapped.

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:49.090
This is the file.

00:20:49.090 --> 00:20:50.430
Exactly what we talked about doing.

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:55.720
The only thing awkward now is that we are
going to have to deal with the fact that we

00:20:55.720 --> 00:21:00.710
have 2 columns of the hit accession number.

00:21:00.710 --> 00:21:03.240
Used to line the two tables up.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:07.110
You can remove that easily with the command
that uses cut.

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:08.840
Cut works with columns.

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:12.210
I can tell it cut this column.

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:13.390
Right here out.

00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:20.060
And use a switch called complement that says
give me everything except that column and

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:22.010
you would get the table back.

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:23.190
That is enough of that.

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:33.390
Let's move on to getting sequences and then
we will finish up.

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:38.440
The final version of that command is right
here in this file on the FTP site.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:40.540
A couple things to mention.

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:49.080
Here are some command lines that I could use
if I wanted to get links to PubMed.

00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:55.320
For one of the records.

00:21:55.320 --> 00:22:01.420
You could do the same kind of thing with the
integration that I did with the other things.

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:03.790
Likewise there is an example for the taxonomy.

00:22:03.790 --> 00:22:11.600
If I wanted to get to lineage from one of
the tax IDs that I had in my table.

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:17.860
They are I have the complete lineage of one
of those tax IDs.

00:22:17.860 --> 00:22:23.870
I could line it up and exactly same way we
lined up the other thing's.

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:30.430
Now we want to use efetch in a different way,
not using XML.

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:33.640
We want to get the protein sequences.

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:36.880
A single record.

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:38.440
Very simple.

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:42.330
The exact same command we had with efetch
before.

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:48.580
Except this time we are going to get the FASTA
format back.

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:50.030
There it is.

00:22:50.030 --> 00:22:54.630
And to get all of them is exactly analogous
to what we did before.

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:58.120
We are going to use epost to do that.

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:02.890
Parse the file as we did before.

00:23:02.890 --> 00:23:04.700
Post it to protein just like we did before.

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:05.910
We are going to efetch it.

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:10.850
We will save it in a file called proteins
dot faa.

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:22.620
What I will od is just let that go down the
screen.

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:27.500
So you can get all the protein sequences that
way.

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:31.420
And that is basically what I wanted to cover
today.

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:35.390
There is an infinite number of things you
can do.

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:39.830
Certainly there are more sophisticated things
you can do if you want to get into parsing

00:23:39.830 --> 00:23:40.830
output.

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:43.920
You can parse one of the structured formats.

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:48.720
This is a simple thing you can do with the
tab limited output.

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:52.880
Once you have it on your computer and have
additional tools to work with, you can do

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:57.530
pretty much anything you want with it anytime
