00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:19.160
Let's open it up for a few questions.

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:21.250
We will probably run a little over time.

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:23.570
I will leave it open for a couple minutes.

00:24:23.570 --> 00:24:32.030
[Rana] A user writes in, these tools are awesome
but there is a question about the number.

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:37.420
Does NCBI block the request when the number
of IDs are very high?

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:41.410
Someone has been having an issue with taxonomy.

00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:46.420
[Peter] So the answer to the question do we
block people?

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:49.190
We do sometimes.

00:24:49.190 --> 00:24:56.250
But there is another problem that these programs
like efetch in particular, there are basic

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:59.880
physical limits to the number of IDs it can
process.

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:06.600
So if you are in the areas of 100,000s, your
request probably won't work.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:11.980
If you are going to do that kind of retrieval,
you will want to work with smaller amounts.

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:17.100
If you do things like submit multiple requests
simultaneously where you're hitting the Web

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:23.730
servers thousands of times a minute or something
like that, yes, we will block you based on

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:24.730
the IP.

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:27.880
But efetch will fail with large numbers of
IDs.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:33.630
[Rana] For fairly experienced scripter they
have a question is epost better than just

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:34.860
a bash loop?

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:37.720
[peter] I'm not sure I understand what you're
trying to say.

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:45.371
If you are trying to submit one of the things
that we really don't like people doing, there

00:25:45.371 --> 00:25:50.350
are many people do this, they use scripts
and they submit IDs one at a time.

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:54.120
We don't want you to do that if that is what
you mean by a batch loop.

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:59.120
What we want you to do is use epost and submit
the IDs in batches and get them down that

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:00.420
way.

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:06.850
[Rana] Is there an alternative to EDirect
tools to retrieve data from local copies or

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:10.530
are there mirrors of some of the NCBI databases.

00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:14.090
An alternative to EDirect for retrieval?

00:26:14.090 --> 00:26:16.730
[Peter] So no.

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:21.360
EDirect tools are bascially wrappers around
our URL API.

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:26.520
They are accessing the entrez system at NCBI.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:29.040
You can't install that locally.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:31.370
There are no mirrors of that system anywhere.

00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:34.060
I hope that answers your question.

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:39.660
If you have your own local database of things,
you would be using some kind of database program

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:42.260
like SQL to access that database.

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:45.520
I think that is all we have time for.

